There's a particular kind of stillness that follows a long trip. You come back from the hills, the bike still smells of diesel and cold air, and for a few days the ordinary rhythm of office logins and testing tickets feels unbearably flat. I felt this most recently after a trek near Ooty — not sadness exactly, more like a low hum of *now what*. Philosophy has a name for this feeling, though it rarely offers comfort so much as company.

## Two Things That Are Not the Same Problem

For a while I conflated two separate struggles: a job that doesn't excite me, and a fear of being seen. They felt like one large fog of stuckness. But they aren't the same weather system at all.

The Stoics were oddly precise about this. Epictetus opens his *Enchiridion* with a division that sounds almost too simple: some things are up to us, some things are not. My team lead's expectations, his manner of critique, whether the testing work assigned to me is interesting — these live mostly outside my control. What lives inside it is how I prepare, what I choose to learn from a mistake, whether I let someone else's vague feedback become the final verdict on my competence. The moment I stopped trying to fix *him* and started building a checklist from my own code-review mistakes, the problem became mine again — smaller, but actually solvable.

The second struggle — the fear of posting a reel, of putting a ride video into the world — is a completely different animal. It has nothing to do with skill or preparation. I've filmed the footage. I've built an entire bank of ideas in my "Reel idea" note. The block isn't upstream, at the planning stage. It sits exactly at the threshold of visibility — the second before the upload button is pressed.

## The Locus Where Evaluation Happens

Here philosophy offers a second useful distinction, one that sits close to the Stoic idea but isn't quite identical: *internal versus external locus of evaluation*. It isn't wrong to care what people think — that's just being human, and pretending otherwise is its own kind of dishonesty. The actual danger is subtler: it's when the anticipated verdict of an audience gets to author the action *before* the action happens. The follower count decides whether the ride was worth filming. The imagined scroll-past decides whether the reel is good enough to post.

Marcus Aurelius wrote his *Meditations* as private notes to himself, never intending an audience — which is maybe the deepest irony available to someone building a public Instagram presence. But his insight still holds: the work has to be authored from inside first. Whether it lands is a separate, later question, and not one that gets a vote in whether you begin.

## An Empirical Practice, Disguised as a Journal

What I find genuinely rare in my own life right now is the "Fear and Result" note — a running log of predicted outcomes against what actually happened. This is, without much exaggeration, a personal empiricism practice. It's the same method behind cognitive-behavioral therapy's thought records, and it's also just good epistemics applied to the self: don't trust the fear's forecast, test it.

Every time the log shows a feared outcome that didn't materialize, it chips away at the fear's credibility as a predictor. Not through willpower or pep talk, but through evidence. This is quietly more powerful than confidence-building exercises, because it doesn't ask me to *feel* brave — it asks me to notice that the fear has a bad track record.

## The Road as the Test

The planned ride from Delhi to Bangalore, through Gwalior, Nagpur, and Hyderabad, sits ahead as more than a trip. It's shaping up as the natural occasion where preparation, fear, and the threshold of visibility all meet at once. There's a old idea, older than the Stoics, that a journey undertaken alone is also always a journey inward — the road doesn't just move you through geography, it moves you through yourself.

Maybe the honest ambition isn't to arrive at the destination fearless and post everything effortlessly. Maybe it's simpler: log the prediction, take the ride, film what happens, post one thing, and let the log record — once more — that the fear's forecast was wrong.

That, more than any single reel or any single follower count, would be the real trip.
